# Why most published findings are false
subtitle: "A thousand hypotheses in one table show how honest studies, low power and long-shot ideas fill journals with false findings."

## Why most published findings are false
use: two_by_two_table
with:
    row_label: The hypothesis
    col_label: The study says
    row_values:
      - 'True: 100'
      - 'False: 900'
    col_values:
      - Significant
      - Not significant
    cells:
      - ['80', '20']
      - ['45', '855']
sources:
    - id: ioannidis2005
      title: Why Most Published Research Findings Are False
      authors: Ioannidis
      year: 2005
      doi: 10.1371/journal.pmed.0020124
      finding: Low power and low priors make most positive findings false

    math fdr cites ["ioannidis2005"] tex "\\frac{45}{80 + 45} = 36\\%" colors {"45":"#b5482e"} x 865 y 290 hidden true
    label l_fdr "false, of the significant" above #b5482e attach_to fdr hidden true
    portrait portrait_john_ioannidis x 885 y 615 width 105 of "John Ioannidis" hidden true
    text lp00 "20" x 385 y 435 font_size 32 hidden true
    text lp01 "80" x 615 y 435 font_size 32 hidden true
    text ls00 "8" x 385 y 435 font_size 32 hidden true
    text ls01 "2" x 615 y 435 font_size 32 hidden true
    text ls10 "50" x 385 y 285 font_size 32 hidden true
    text ls11 "940" x 615 y 285 font_size 32 hidden true
    text ls_h0 "True: 10" #8f887c x 172 y 435 font_size 28 hidden true
    text ls_h1 "False: 990" #8f887c x 172 y 285 font_size 28 hidden true
    group lowpow members ["lp00","lp01"] hidden true
    group longshot members ["ls00","ls01","ls10","ls11","ls_h0","ls_h1"] hidden true
    text rt "1,000 hypotheses" #8f887c x 140 y 500 font_size 26 hidden true
    text rh0 "True: 100" #8f887c x 172 y 435 font_size 28 hidden true
    text rh1 "False: 900" #8f887c x 172 y 285 font_size 28 hidden true
    text ch0 "Significant" #8f887c x 385 y 545 font_size 28 hidden true
    text ch1 "Not significant" #8f887c x 615 y 545 font_size 28 hidden true
    text ct "The study says" #8f887c x 500 y 600 font_size 26 hidden true
    group heads members ["rt","rh0","rh1","ch0","ch1","ct"] hidden true
    path pubbox dashed #b5482e points [[262,202],[508,202],[508,518],[262,518],[262,202]] hidden true
    text publab "published" #b5482e x 385 y 172 font_size 30 hidden true

    card title "Why most published findings are false"

    hide row_title col_title row_header_0 row_header_1 col_header_0 col_header_1

How can most published findings be false when nobody cheats? Sort a thousand hypotheses a field might test: true or false, significant or not.
    draw grid heads

Say a hundred of the thousand are really true. That share, the prior, is the number no single study reports.
    highlight rh0 rh1

Studies with eighty percent power find eighty of the hundred true effects, and miss twenty.
    draw cell_0_0 cell_0_1

The nine hundred false hypotheses face a five percent threshold, so forty-five come out significant anyway, by chance.
    draw cell_1_0 cell_1_1

    pause 0.4

Journals print the significant column: a hundred and twenty-five findings.
    group pubmark members ["pubbox","publab"]

Forty-five of them are false: more than one in three, though every study followed the rules.
    draw fdr l_fdr

    erase cell_0_0 cell_0_1

    draw lowpow

Now make the studies small, with twenty percent power. Only twenty true effects surface, but the forty-five false ones stay.
    morph fdr tex "\\frac{45}{20 + 45} = 69\\%"

Two in three significant findings are now false. Low power doesn't just miss effects; it makes the effects you do find less believable.
    highlight fdr part "20"

    erase lowpow cell_1_0 cell_1_1 rh0 rh1

    draw longshot

And take a field chasing long shots, where only ten hypotheses in a thousand are true, tested with good power again.
    morph fdr tex "\\frac{50}{8 + 50} = 86\\%"

Eight true finds, fifty false ones: most of what it publishes is false.
    highlight fdr

John Ioannidis set out this arithmetic in 2005, in a paper whose title makes the claim outright.
    draw portrait_john_ioannidis

    erase portrait_john_ioannidis

So a significant result is only as believable as its field's prior and its study's power, and a p-value reports neither.
    highlight ls_h0

    quiz "A field of long shots, tested with large studies: are its significant findings mostly true or mostly false?"
        right: Mostly false: when true hypotheses are rare, the five percent of false ones that slip through outnumber the real finds, whatever the power.
        * Mostly true: the power is high
        + Mostly false: few hypotheses are true
        * Exactly 5% false
